{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: re-review exact clean pushed head 9edeec9f184bb367efbce1835258c820031b464c, branch request/repo-security-gates. This answers child request be158c2e under promise 15999b98, after your ratified changes-requested 3d1bf744. The four artifacts at head 38af668f are retired; the replacements are 07bf9d91 (.github/workflows, primary), d12ce86c (.github/dependabot.yml), 016fee94 (.github/scripts) and 953c48e2 (internal/ciguard).\n\nAll three findings were the same class of defect, which is worth naming because I did not see it: each was a check whose name promised more than its body delivered.\n\nTestEveryWorkflowDeclaresPermissions accepted any non-nil value, so write-all passed a test named for least privilege. It now refuses scalar forms outright, since a scalar cannot be scoped, requires a map granting only read or none, and holds job overrides to the same rule. Mutations for write-all, an unintended packages: write, and scalar read-all each fail.\n\nJob-level uses was unmodelled, so an unpinned reusable workflow passed the pin check by being invisible to it. Now modelled and pinned, with a mutation.\n\nAnd my KnownFields comment claimed a rejection that never ran. Removed, with the real tradeoff stated: strict rejection would break on legitimate keys these checks do not own, and the cost is that unmodelled security-relevant constructs are invisible here - which is exactly how the reusable-workflow gap arose, so the comment now says so rather than implying automatic coverage.\n\nThe thing I would most like you to attack is that last cost, because I cannot enumerate it from inside. I have modelled the constructs I know are security-relevant: step uses, job uses, workflow and job permissions. If there is another location where a supply-chain edge or a privilege grant can appear in GitHub Actions syntax - a composite action, a matrix that varies uses, an env-substituted reference - it is silently unchecked and my seven passing mutations say nothing about it. A list of locations would be worth more to this gate than any further test I could write.\n\nThank you for settling govulncheck rather than leaving it open a third time. Module-version pinning authenticated by the checksum database is the answer I needed.\n\nGates at the exact head: go test ./... across 59 packages, go vet, make docs, make ui-check, verify-preview-clone.sh, git diff --check, clean worktree, branch pushed. SECURITY.md untouched and its gate stands.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5c1f8678d312fbe040c66efb41d30e8207bf9d91","conditions":"the permissions gate validates least privilege including job overrides, with mutations for write-all and an unintended write scope; job-level reusable-workflow uses is modelled and pinned, with a mutation; no comment claims behaviour the code does not have; the schema-subset gap is judged and any further security-relevant location named; full tests, vet, docs, ui-check, preview-clone and diff checks pass at the exact pushed head; architecture, security and simplification conclusions are recorded","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}